", "private AI platform"], "faq": [["Why would a telecom operator run its own LLMs?", "Network configurations, customer records and incident data are among its most sensitive assets, and as an essential entity under NIS2 it must manage supply-chain and security risk tightly. Running capable open-weight models in-house keeps that data inside its own controls."], ["Does self-hosting mean giving up hosted models entirely?", "No. In this design, non-sensitive tasks can use hosted models through the same gateway, while sensitive tasks are routed only to in-house models. The gateway enforces which data can go where."], ["What is the hardest part of running an in-house LLM platform?", "Operations: keeping multi-GPU serving healthy through hardware and driver failures, sharing scarce GPUs fairly between teams, and proving quality and cost per task."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto-europe/", "/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/"], "citations": [{"title": "Mistral releases weights for its trillion-parameter AI model, targeting cybersecurity", "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/29/mistral-releases-weights-for-its-trillion-parameter-ai-model-targeting-cybersecurity/", "publisher": "TechCrunch", "date": "29 Oct 2026"}, {"title": "NIS 2 directive: transposition status and what companies must do", "url": "https://www.wavestone.com/en/insight/nis-2-european-countries-transposing-directive/", "publisher": "Wavestone", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "EU takes member states to court over unimplemented cybersecurity law", "url": "https://therecord.media/eu-cyber-filing-ireland-spain-france-netherlands-nis2", "publisher": "The Record", "date": "Jul 2026"}, {"title": "NIS2 Directive", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NIS2_Directive", "publisher": "Wikipedia"}, {"title": "Navigating Failures in Pods With Devices", "url": "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/07/03/navigating-failures-in-pods-with-devices/", "publisher": "Kubernetes blog", "date": "3 Jul 2025"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A telecom operator in central Europe ran fixed and mobile networks for several million customers. Three teams wanted generative AI: network operations, to read alarms and configuration changes; customer care, to summarise interactions and draft replies; and security, to triage alerts and investigate incidents. Each had started its own pilot with a different hosted model, and the security and legal teams had stopped all three because network topology, customer data and incident details were leaving the company.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> Telecom operators are essential entities under the EU's NIS2 Directive, with duties covering risk management, supply-chain security and incident reporting<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Transposition has been uneven, and the Commission took several member states to the Court of Justice in July 2026 for not implementing it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>, but national laws are now arriving; Austria's, for example, applies from 1 October 2026<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. At the same time, capable open-weight models became realistic to run in-house; on 29 October 2026 Mistral released the weights of its trillion-parameter Large 4, pitched at security teams who want to run it on their own infrastructure<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The operator decided to build one shared, sovereign platform rather than three pilots.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sensitive data stays in-house</strong>: network, customer and incident data only touch the operator's own infrastructure.</li>
<li><strong>One platform, three tenants</strong>, with quotas, policies and costs kept separate.</li>
<li><strong>Models chosen by evidence</strong>: evaluations on each team's real tasks.</li>
<li><strong>Reliable on imperfect hardware</strong>: GPU, driver and node failures must not take services down.</li>
<li><strong>A sensible escape hatch</strong>: hosted models for non-sensitive work, through the same controls.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Tenants</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Network operations<em>alarms, configs</em></span><span>Customer care<em>summaries, drafts</em></span><span>Security<em>triage, investigation</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Gateway</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Inference gateway<em>auth, quotas, routing, data rules</em></span><span>Classification check<em>sensitive data stays in-house</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Serving</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">vLLM pools<em>large, medium and small models</em></span><span>Hosted fallback<em>non-sensitive only</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Compute</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Kubernetes GPU nodes<em>DRA, health checks</em></span><span>Model registry<em>versions, licences</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Assure</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Evaluations<em>per tenant, per release</em></span><span>Tracing and cost<em>OpenTelemetry</em></span><span>Security controls<em>NIS2 evidence</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Tenants never call models directly; the gateway decides which model, and where, each request may use.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Request</b><span>A tenant application sends a task with its data classification</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Check</b><span>The gateway verifies identity, quota and whether data may leave the platform</span></li>
<li><b>Route</b><span>The task goes to the smallest in-house model that meets quality for that task</span></li>
<li><b>Serve</b><span>vLLM serves from GPU pools; failures move work to healthy replicas</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Trace</b><span>Tokens, latency, model version and cost are recorded per tenant</span></li>
<li><b>Fallback</b><span>Only non-sensitive tasks may use a hosted model when capacity is short</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Data classification decides where a request may go before any model sees it.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: running GPUs like a utility</h2>
<p>Buying GPUs was the easy part. Keeping them useful was not. Multi-GPU model servers are sensitive to hardware faults, driver updates and slow terminations, and Kubernetes' traditional view of devices as either present or absent does not cope well with partial failures. The Kubernetes community has documented these failure modes, from device plugin restarts to pods holding devices while they shut down<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/eu-telecom-sovereign-llm-platform/device-failures.webp" alt="Diagram of kubelet, device plugin, node status and a user pod, annotated with failure points: kubelet restarts losing device information, device plugin updates or restarts preventing allocation, and slow pod termination leaving devices unavailable" width="1200" height="514" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Where device-backed workloads break on Kubernetes: the failure modes the platform was designed around. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/07/03/navigating-failures-in-pods-with-devices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Sergey Kanzhelev and Mrunal Patel</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The team designed for those failures: health checks on GPUs and model servers, spare capacity so a failed node does not halve a model pool, staged driver and plugin updates, and a gateway that retries on a healthy replica. Scheduling moved to Dynamic Resource Allocation so model servers could ask for the exact accelerators they needed; see <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">DRA for GPU scheduling</a>. Sharing used quotas per tenant, with security given priority during incidents.</p>
<p>Model choice was the other discipline. The largest open models are impressive and expensive to hold in memory. Each tenant built an evaluation set from real tasks, and the gateway routed each task to the smallest model that met its quality bar, keeping the largest model for hard security investigations. More in <a href="/insights/mistral-large-4-open-weights-self-hosting/">running a trillion-parameter model yourself</a> and <a href="/insights/ai-memory-squeeze-inference-architecture/">the AI memory squeeze</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Self-hosting is an operations commitment, not a download. Budget for spare capacity, failure handling and evaluation before you budget for the biggest model.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform team</small><ol><li>GPU cluster and DRA</li><li class="hl">Gateway and quotas</li><li>Failure drills</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Tenant teams</small><ol><li>Use cases</li><li class="hl">Evaluation sets</li><li>Adoption</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Security and legal</small><ol><li>Data classification</li><li>Model licences</li><li class="hl">NIS2 evidence</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The platform team ran it like any other shared utility; tenants owned their quality bar.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Gate</b><ul><li>Gateway with data rules</li><li>Hosted models for safe tasks</li><li>Pilots consolidated</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Build</b><ul><li>GPU nodes</li><li>vLLM pools</li><li>Evaluation sets</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Harden</b><ul><li>Failure handling</li><li>Quotas and priorities</li><li>Cost per task</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Scale</b><ul><li>More tenants</li><li>Larger models where earned</li><li>Quarterly model review</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The gateway came first, so every later change was invisible to tenant applications.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One shared platform</td><td>A cluster per team</td><td>Scarce GPUs used well; one set of controls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gateway enforces data classification</td><td>Trust each application</td><td>One place to prove where data went</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smallest model that passes evaluation</td><td>Largest model everywhere</td><td>Lower memory, cost and latency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spare capacity for failures</td><td>Run GPUs at full allocation</td><td>Service survives node and driver failures</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hosted fallback for non-sensitive tasks</td><td>In-house only</td><td>Handles peaks without overbuying hardware</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: put the gateway in first. It stopped the data leaks on day one and let the platform change underneath without tenant rework. Differently: run failure drills before the first production tenant, not after the first outage. Related: <a href="/insights/kubernetes-inference-gateway/">Kubernetes inference gateway</a>, <a href="/insights/llm-inference-serving-vllm/">serving with vLLM</a>, <a href="/case-studies/telecom-observability-slos/">telecom observability and SLOs</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</p>
<p>If you are planning private AI infrastructure in Europe or elsewhere, see my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
